<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sinhala Only Act</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1956">1956</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Official Language Act No. 33 of 1956</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Sinhala Only Act, formally the Official Language Act No. 33 of 1956, was passed by the Parliament of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceylon">Ceylon</a> under Prime Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/s-w-r-d-bandaranaike">S. W. R. D. Bandaranaike</a>. It replaced English with Sinhala as the sole official language, excluding Tamil, and many Tamil-speaking public servants lost their posts. It deepened the divide between Sinhalese and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamils">Tamils</a> that later fed the Sri Lankan Civil War.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Under British rule English was the official language of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceylon">Ceylon</a>, and English schools founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaffna">Jaffna</a> by the American Ceylon Mission gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamils">Tamils</a> there a larger share of civil service posts than their share of the population: by 1946 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lankan-tamils">Sri Lankan Tamils</a>, 11% of the people, held 33% of clerical jobs. In 1936 and in the 1940s leaders such as N. M. Perera, Philip Gunawardena, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/j-r-jayewardene">J. R. Jayewardene</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/s-w-r-d-bandaranaike">S. W. R. D. Bandaranaike</a> supported Sinhala and Tamil together as official languages. After Bandaranaike broke with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-national-party">United National Party</a> in 1951 and founded the Sri Lanka Freedom Party, the call for parity gave way by 1953 to the cry of Sinhala only, and the party campaigned on it from 1955.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>After the landslide of the Mahajana Eksath Peramuna in the 1956 election, Bandaranaike at first planned administrative provisions for Tamil, but a fast unto death by K. M. P. Rajaratne and F. R. Jayasuriya made him drop them. The bill passed with the votes of his party and the UNP, against the Lanka Sama Samaja Party, the Communist Party and the Tamil parties. The Tamil Language (Special Provisions) Act of 1958 allowed Tamil in education, public service examinations and the administration of the Northern and Eastern provinces, but its regulations were not approved until 1966, while the Language of the Courts Act of 1961 made Sinhala the only language of the courts.</p><p>Tamil officers, even in Tamil areas, had to learn Sinhala. Tamils held 30 percent of the administrative service and 50 percent of the clerical service in 1956, but 5 percent of each by 1970.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Supporters saw the act as a break with colonial rule, opponents as the oppression of minorities by the linguistic majority. In Parliament Colvin R. de Silva warned: &quot;One language, two nations; Two languages, one Nation.&quot; Tamil protesters of the Federal Party held a satyagraha outside Parliament, and a mob of the Eksath Bhikkhu Peramuna attacked them; the Gal Oya riots of 5 and 6 June 1956 killed nearly 150 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamils">Tamils</a>. For Tamils the act became a justification for the demand for Tamil Eelam. In 1987, under Indian pressure, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirteenth-amendment">Thirteenth Amendment</a> made Tamil also an official language, with English as a link language.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sinhala Only Act” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>S. W. R. D. Bandaranaike won the 1956 election and passed the <b>Sinhala Only Act</b>, deepening tension with Tamils.</blockquote><a href="/state/dominion-of-ceylon">Dominion of Ceylon</a></li></ul></section>
